"""
A text run carrying ONE non-ASCII codepoint must wrap from the measured font
advances, exactly like its ASCII twin.

`resolved_font_advances` carries one advance per CODEPOINT; every wrap offset
in this renderer is a BYTE index. Two separate defects followed from that, and
this spec pins both:

  1. `_lay_compute_style_wrap_ranges_inner` gated the measured-advance path on
     `resolved_font_advances.len() != txt.len()` — a codepoint count against a
     byte count. One `&mdash;` (3 bytes, 1 codepoint) made the two disagree, so
     the whole wrap fell back to `compute_wrap_ranges`, the flat cells-per-line
     estimate (`style_char_w` = 12 px at font-size 16, against a real ~7.8 px
     average for this face). Being ~50 % too wide, the estimate wrapped such a
     run one line early, and every box after it inherited the 24 px step.

  2. `style_run_byte_advances` — the byte-indexed table that exists to answer
     exactly this — was itself dead on non-ASCII. It expanded codepoints to
     bytes by classifying `char_code_at(i)` as a UTF-8 lead or continuation
     byte, but `char_code_at` DECODES: it never returns a value in 128..191, so
     no continuation byte was ever recognised, `ci` over-ran the advance array
     on the first multi-byte codepoint, and the function returned an EMPTY
     table. Both layout and paint then fell back together and silently.

Measured against headless Chrome (`--headless=new --window-size=900,20000`) on
the fixture below, a 300 px block, 16px/1.5 sans-serif:

| run                                   | Chrome | Simple before | Simple after |
|---------------------------------------|--------|---------------|--------------|
| `alpha — beta gamma … tau` (em dash)   | 72     | 120           | 72           |
| `alpha xx beta gamma … tau` (ASCII)    | 72     | 72            | 72 (control) |

The ASCII row is the control that must NOT move: it already took the measured
path, and a fix that changed it would mean the byte table disagrees with the
codepoint table on the runs where they must agree.

Not asserted here, still open and recorded rather than guessed at: a mixed
inline run (`<code>` + text) inside a NARROW block still measures one line tall
too many — an inline-packing question, not a measurement one, and untouched by
this change.
"""

"""
Traceability: REQ-BROWSER-ENGINE-TEXT-WRAP-004 — a text run containing non-ASCII codepoints wraps from the measured per-glyph advances, not the flat cell-width estimate.
"""
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/browser_engine/simple_web_html_layout_renderer_layout.spl 1%
use std.spec.*
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_html_layout_renderer.{
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id
}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_html_layout_renderer_layout.{
    utf8_encoded_len, next_codepoint_start
}

val VW: i32 = 900
val VH: i32 = 20000
val HEAD: text = "<html><head><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;font:16px/1.5 sans-serif}#x{width:300px}</style></head><body>"

val EM_DASH_RUN: text = "alpha — beta gamma delta epsilon zeta eta theta iota kappa lambda mu nu xi omicron pi rho sigma tau"
val ASCII_RUN: text = "alpha xx beta gamma delta epsilon zeta eta theta iota kappa lambda mu nu xi omicron pi rho sigma tau"

fn probe(inner: text, id: text, name: text) -> text:
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id(
        HEAD + inner + "</body></html>", VW, VH, id, name)

fn block_height(body_text: text) -> text:
    probe("<div id=\"x\">" + body_text + "</div>", "x", "h")

describe "non-ASCII text runs wrap from measured advances":
    it "AC-1: an em-dash run is Chrome's 3 lines, not the 5 the flat estimate gave":
        step("one U+2014 in a 300 px block; Chrome measures 72 px")
        expect(block_height(EM_DASH_RUN)).to_equal("72")

    it "AC-2 (control): the ASCII twin is unchanged at 72":
        step("same token structure, no non-ASCII codepoint — already correct")
        expect(block_height(ASCII_RUN)).to_equal("72")

    it "AC-3: the two runs agree, which is the property the defect broke":
        step("a single em dash must not cost two extra line boxes")
        expect(block_height(EM_DASH_RUN)).to_equal(block_height(ASCII_RUN))

    it "AC-4: a bare em dash still occupies exactly one line":
        step("the round-14 regression shape: one glyph must not wrap per byte")
        expect(block_height("—")).to_equal("24")

    it "AC-5: utf8_encoded_len covers every UTF-8 width":
        step("the expansion table the byte-advance array is built from")
        expect(utf8_encoded_len(65)).to_equal(1)
        expect(utf8_encoded_len(0xE9)).to_equal(2)
        expect(utf8_encoded_len(0x2014)).to_equal(3)
        expect(utf8_encoded_len(0x1F600)).to_equal(4)

    it "AC-6: next_codepoint_start never stops inside a codepoint":
        step("the forced-progress step of the wrap loop")
        expect(next_codepoint_start("a—b", 0)).to_equal(1)
        expect(next_codepoint_start("a—b", 1)).to_equal(4)
        expect(next_codepoint_start("a—b", 4)).to_equal(5)
